    "TREATMENT": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Topical cyclosporine or tacrolimus—rarely irritating, but if noted consider compounded",
          "pdf_page": 882,
          "printed_page": 800,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Outpatient—unless severe corneal aqueous preparation. ulceration.",
          "pdf_page": 882,
          "printed_page": 800,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Pilocarpine—initially irritating topically;",
          "pdf_page": 882,
          "printed_page": 800,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Instruct client to use solution(s) before systemic side effect risk. ointment(s) and wait 5 minutes between",
          "pdf_page": 882,
          "printed_page": 800,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Topical corticosteroids—avoid with treatments. ulcerative keratitis or severe KCS (corneal",
          "pdf_page": 882,
          "printed_page": 800,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Advise client to call at once if ocular pain ulcer risk). increases (patients are predisposed to corneal ulceration).",
          "pdf_page": 882,
          "printed_page": 800,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Parotid duct transposition—surgical option for dogs and cats that reroutes parotid duct to deliver saliva to ocular surface if KCS is refractory to lacrimostimulant therapy.",
          "pdf_page": 882,
          "printed_page": 800,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Regular rechecks—monitor response and",
          "pdf_page": 882,
          "printed_page": 800,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "text": "More common with congenital KCS.",
          "pdf_page": 882,
          "printed_page": 800,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "progress.",
          "pdf_page": 882,
          "printed_page": 800,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "text": "Saliva can irritate cornea and result in",
          "pdf_page": 882,
          "printed_page": 800,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Schirmer tear test—4–6 weeks after mineral deposits; some patients require initiating cyclosporine or tacrolimus (patient ongoing topical therapy. should receive drug as prescribed on day of",
          "pdf_page": 882,
          "printed_page": 800,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Episcleral cyclosporine implant—for dogs visit). responsive to topical cyclosporine but with",
          "pdf_page": 882,
          "printed_page": 800,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Usually requires life-long treatment. poor owner compliance.",
